AB  - The shift towards semantic-driven architectures in edge networks requires innovative scheduling paradigms that bridge the gap between physical resources and task semantics. This paper presents a Knowledge-Driven Task Scheduling (KDTS) framework designed to address this challenge by integrating physical networks with knowledge graphs for semantic representation. The framework incorporates dynamic knowledge evolution through entity expansion and confidence-aware relation updates, along with dual-timescale optimization, combining KG-enhanced A∗search at the millisecond level and policy gradients at the minute level. Extensive simulations demonstrate that, under resource contention, KDTS achieves a 92% success rate with a 95% confidence interval of [0.90, 0.94], and reduces queue delay by 40.6%, reaching 3.4 seconds compared to baseline methods. In high-semantic scenarios, KDTS improves KGScore by 56.1% (from 0.52 to 0.81) and reduces latency by 40.3% (down to 4.3ms). Under extreme load conditions, KDTS maintains sub-5.2ms latency with only 13.1% knowledge overhead, demonstrating its robustness in semantic-aware networks.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
