TI  - A Self-Evolving Cognitive Neuro-Symbolic Architecture for Continually Adaptive Medical Diagnosis
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Circuit, Power and Computing Technologies, ICCPCT 2026
SP  - 75
EP  - 80
DO  - 10.1109/ICCPCT70290.2026.11654852
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049009719?origin=resultslist
AB  - Artificial intelligence has achieved remarkable success in clinical diagnosis, particularly through multimodal architectures and large-scale language models trained on extensive medical corpora. Despite these advances, most deployed systems rely on fixed inference pathways and implicitly presume that clinical data distributions remain stable. In real healthcare settings, however, patient states evolve over time, rare conditions emerge unpredictably, and discrepancies frequently arise between structured records, laboratory measurements, and imaging findings. These dynamic characteristics expose limitations in models designed primarily for static prediction. To address this challenge, we develop a neuro-symbolic diagnostic framework capable of restructuring its internal reasoning process when confronted with changing or contradictory medical evidence. Rather than depending exclusively on parameter retraining, the proposed architecture incorporates explicit cross-modal inconsistency quantification, reinforcement-guided reasoning adaptation, and a stability-plasticity-based memory consolidation mechanism that preserves previously acquired knowledge during adaptation. A dynamically updated knowledge graph supports causal traceability, enabling interpretable and logically grounded diagnostic inference. Empirical assessment on the MIMIC-IV intensive care dataset together with a publicly available chest radiography benchmark demonstrates quantifiable improvements in diagnostic performance and adaptability. The framework improves overall diagnostic accuracy by approximately 4-5% (achieving 93.8% accuracy), increases rare-class F1 performance by 9.7%, enhances contradiction stabilization reliability by 16.4%, and reduces catastrophic forgetting under sequential updates by 8.2%. These results suggest that enabling structured reasoning reconfiguration-rather than refining static prediction alone-substantially strengthens robustness in evolving clinical environments. © 2026 IEEE.
