TI  - Semantic Management of TinyML for Industrial Application
PY  - 2026
T2  - Tiny Machine Learning: Design Principles and Applications
SP  - 469
EP  - 502
DO  - 10.1002/9781394294572.ch13
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032594240?origin=resultslist
AB  - By fostering the collaborative design of resource-constrained Internet of Things (IoT) hardware devices and their corresponding learning-based software architectures, tiny machine learning (TinyML) plays a pivotal role in the ongoing fourth and fifth industrial revolutions, enabling individuals, economies, and societies to harness the power of efficient AI-driven computing technologies, such as medical robotics, autonomous vehicles, and smart cities. Given its inherently interdisciplinary nature, TinyML has been explored from a variety of perspectives. However, the hardware and software limitations pose significant challenges for TinyML. The small memory capacity and the complex deep learning models designed for cloud platforms are difficult to implement on such constrained devices, including mobile platforms. Additionally, the support for compilers and inference engines is limited, especially on bare-metal devices. To overcome these challenges, a co-design approach to both the system stack and algorithms is essential. This chapter aims to provide an up-to-date overview, emphasizing the diverse learning methods employed in TinyML-based solutions. In this research, a framework is presented that leverages Semantic Web technologies to enable the large-scale, collaborative management of TinyML models and IoT devices. This approach spans from data modeling to identifying potential pairings and benchmarks, ultimately facilitating the seamless exchange and repurposing of TinyML components. A semantic schema for neural network models aligns with the World Wide Web Consortium’s Thing Description, offering a semantic representation of IoT devices. Additionally, it introduces a publicly accessible knowledge graph consisting of 23 models. To demonstrate the approach, three case studies involving ML models and six IoT devices are discussed. © 2026 by The Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers, Inc. All rights reserved.
