TI  - Benchmarking Machine Learning and Generative AI Paradigms for Automated Test Case Design From Industrial Requirements
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Machine Intelligence and Smart Innovation, ICMISI 2026 - Proceedings
SP  - 455
EP  - 459
DO  - 10.1109/ICMISI69868.2026.11584228
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045233573?origin=resultslist
AB  - The accelerating complexity of contemporary software architectures characterized by distributed microservices, heterogeneous data flows, and dynamically rendered user interfaces has rendered conventional, human-driven test case design both operationally unsustainable and structurally inadequate. Whereas Artificial Intelligence (AI) has demonstrated considerable promise in automating downstream code synthesis tasks, the upstream challenge of converting ambiguous, natural-language industrial requirements into logically sound, traceable test specifications remains largely unresolved. This paper presents a structured empirical comparison of three computational paradigms: a classical Machine Learning baseline (Random Forest), a Generative AI pipeline augmented with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), and a high-parameter static Large Language Model (Mistral 7B). Each architecture is systematically evaluated against authentic, enterprise-scale requirement specifications derived from Misr Travel Agency a leading Egyptian travel and tourism platform. The study quantifies critical failure modes including hallucination proliferation, semantic traceability degradation, and the computational overhead associated with high-fidelity reasoning. Empirical outcomes reveal a definitive inverse relationship between generation throughput and logical fidelity, establishing the necessity of context-anchored generation strategies for any AI system aspiring to autonomous test design at industrial scale.  © 2026 IEEE.
