TI  - Enhancing Large Language Model (LLM) Reliability: A Comparative Analysis of RAPTOR, GraphRAG, and Agentic Strategies for Domain Specific QA
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Information Science and Communication Technology, ICISCT 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICISCT67740.2026.11576401
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044307998?origin=resultslist
AB  - Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) is a common technique to connect large language models (LLM) with private data. However, the standard RAG setup has major problems with complex reasoning. It usually forgets the broader context of the entire document and fails to connect the important details that appear on different pages. To solve these issues, this study compares three new approaches: RAPTOR (Recursive summarization), GraphRAG (knowledge graphs), and AgenticRAG (autonomous planning). We built local pipelines for all three methods using Python's LlamaIndex framework. Instead of using an easy text for testing, we used a complex document: Tesla's 10 K financial report, which is over 100 pages long. Our goal was to test the three pipelines on three different tasks: creating high level summaries, linking hidden facts across different sections, and solving complex math problems. The results indicate that performance varies significantly by task. RAPTOR proved to be the most effective for high level summaries, while GraphRAG achieved the highest accuracy for mapping relationships between entities. Agentic RAG was the only architecture capable of solving complex calculation tasks, but it required significantly more processing time. We conclude that there is no single RAG architecture that is universally superior. Instead, engineers must select the specific architecture that aligns with their data structure and reasoning requirements. © 2026 IEEE.
