UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032766846?origin=resultslist
AB  - Embedding models are fundamental to modern Natural Language Processing (NLP), transforming textual data into dense vector representations that capture semantic meaning and relationships. This systematic survey focuses exclusively on the landscape of open-source embedding models, tracing their evolution from early word embeddings (Word2Vec, GloVe) to advanced contextual and multimodal architectures (BERT, Sentence-BERT, BGE, Nomic Embed). Moving beyond descriptive narratives, our core contribution is a structured, comparative analysis of these models’ operational tradeoffs, including model size, embedding dimensionality, inference latency, and memory consumption. This systematic review spans the literature up to 2025 drawing upon 70 papers to provide a critical assessment of standardized benchmarks, such as MTEB, and their limitations regarding bias and robustness. Finally, the paper concludes by discussing the broad applicability of embedding models across diverse domains, including search engines, recommendation systems, fraud detection, and healthcare, and offer recommendations for effective model selection and utilization. © 2013 IEEE.
